/* Copyright 2026 Rhett Creighton - Apache License 2.0
 *
 * EVERY EXTERNAL ENTRY POINT THIS TREE REACHES, AND WHAT IT REACHES FOR.
 *
 * WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. config/capability_classes.def names nine kinds of
 * reach. This file is the other half: it says which of them each external
 * symbol belongs to. Together they turn "what can this code do" into a lookup
 * rather than a judgement. The classes file explains why the table has to be
 * CLOSED; this is where that closure is spent. A symbol with no row here is a
 * build failure, not a symbol that is assumed harmless, so the first time a
 * module calls a C library entry point nobody has classified, the build stops
 * and a human writes one line. That costs one row per new libc call for the
 * whole project, because the table is sized by the C library and by the four
 * vendored libraries this tree links, not by the codebase.
 *
 * SCOPE. Rows cover the undefined symbols that survive linking the tree
 * against itself: the C library, the math library, the dynamic loader,
 * OpenSSL, zlib, LevelDB, SQLite, libsecp256k1 and the embedded Tor. In-tree
 * symbols are never rows, because a call into this repository lands on code
 * the gate already scanned; only the boundary needs naming.
 *
 * CAP_HARMLESS IS NOT A CAPABILITY CLASS AND HAS NO ROW IN
 * config/capability_classes.def. Do not go looking for one; the two files are
 * not disagreeing. That file names nine kinds of REACH, and a class earns its
 * place there by being something an attacker could want. CAP_HARMLESS is the
 * opposite kind of thing: it is a VERDICT this table records about a symbol,
 * saying that the symbol belongs to none of the nine. It lives here because
 * the judgement is per symbol, and it is spelled as a CAP_ id only so that
 * every row in this file has the same shape and the gate can read one column.
 *
 * It is also not CAP_NONE. CAP_NONE is what a SOURCE FILE has when it
 * declares nothing, and the classes file is right that it needs no row,
 * because absence is what makes the module table closed. CAP_HARMLESS is what
 * a SYMBOL has when somebody looked at it and found nothing to declare, and
 * that is a presence, not an absence: it is the row that stops the build from
 * failing. Different subject, opposite mechanism, and the reason both exist is
 * that a missing module row must mean "claims nothing" while a missing symbol
 * row must mean "nobody has looked yet".
 *
 * CAP_HARMLESS IS EARNED, NEVER DEFAULTED. It is the verdict that a symbol
 * opens no descriptor, names no path, moves no byte off the box, reads no
 * clock and changes no authority. memcpy earns it. close earns it, because
 * the acquisition it undoes is what carried the class. open does not earn it
 * and never will. Most rows are CAP_HARMLESS; that reviewed majority is what
 * makes the smaller set of capability-bearing boundary calls worth reading.
 *
 * WHERE ONE SYMBOL SPANS TWO CLASSES, THE RESTRICTIVE ONE WINS. A wrong row
 * in the strict direction costs one declaration; a wrong row in the loose
 * direction costs the property. This is why fopen, open, openat, fdopen,
 * gzopen and mmap are all CAP_FS_READ with a note rather than a class of
 * their own: nm cannot see O_WRONLY, and inventing a read-or-write class
 * would say less than either half does. The flag-level distinction belongs to
 * check-capability-closure, which reads source and can see it.
 *
 * THE TLS ROWS FOLLOW AN EXISTING PRECEDENT. The test
 * lib/test/src/test_cold_join_sovereign.c already asserts that no object
 * in this tree references the TLS-client and trust-store entry points,
 * listing them by name. That test is the authority on where the TLS
 * boundary sits, and none of the symbols it names appear below, because
 * this node is a TLS server and never a client. The server-side twins it
 * does not name are classified CAP_NETWORK here on the same reasoning, so
 * the two files cannot drift into disagreeing.
 *
 * THREE HOLES THAT ROWS CANNOT CLOSE, named so nobody rediscovers them:
 *
 *   - syscall. The number is a runtime value, so no row binds it. It is
 *     CAP_PRIVILEGE here and banned outright at source level.
 *   - Inline assembly and hand-rolled traps. No symbol, nothing to classify.
 *   - Anything dlopen brings in. CAP_DYNLOAD is the sharpest class for that
 *     reason: its subject does not exist when the check runs.
 *
 * Groups are banners, alphabetical within a group, and carry no meaning. The
 * gate reads the class id, never a position.
 */

/* ZCL_CAPABILITY_SYMBOL(symbol, class, note)
 *
 *   symbol  the reviewed external entry-point name. Fortify aliases remain
 *           exact rows where their semantics differ; on MinGW, mechanical
 *           __imp_ / __mingw_ COFF decoration inherits this base judgement
 *           in check-capability-closure rather than duplicating every row
 *   class   one of the CAP_ ids in config/capability_classes.def, or
 *           CAP_HARMLESS
 *   note    one line saying why this class and not the neighbouring one.
 *           Empty where the choice is not arguable.
 */

